SUPPLY CHAIN MORNING - THE WEEKEND READ
Saturday, March 14, 2026

For twenty years the logic of distribution ran one direction: fewer, bigger buildings. A national retailer could serve the country from six million-square-foot facilities, because trucking was cheap, service expectations were loose, and the inventory savings from pooling demand were enormous.

The last three years have bent that logic. Truckload rates are structurally higher than the 2010s, next-day delivery is the baseline for consumer goods, and tariff volatility has made everyone hold more inventory closer to where it sells. The result, visible in the leasing data, is a resurgence of the 200,000-to-400,000-square-foot regional DC in secondary markets: Columbus, Indianapolis, Reno, Savannah's hinterland, the Central Valley.

What makes this round different from the 1990s version is software. A regional network only works if you can see inventory across it and route orders to the right building in real time. That capability used to require a seven-figure warehouse management deployment per site. Now it is a subscription, and the mid-market companies that could never afford the old systems are the ones building regional networks fastest.

There is a labor angle too. A 300,000-square-foot building in Reno can be staffed; a million-square-foot building in the Inland Empire increasingly cannot, at any wage the operator is willing to pay. Automation vendors love the mega-site pitch, but the actual growth in robot deployments is in the regional tier, where the business case is "we cannot hire" rather than "we want to be efficient."

